column2:
[My friend ... ] eminent —
70	[you who have] sense and reason — do you now talk profanity?
71	[Wh]y, my friend, did your heart talk profanity [ ... ?]
72	[The dream was pr]ecious, and the terror of it great;
73	[your lips(?)] are twitching like flies;
74	[the dread is g]reat, but the dream is a rare thing.
75	To the liv[in]g one it left mourning:
76	[the dea]d left misery to the one who lives on.
77	[I will pl]ead, I will pray to the great gods;
78	I will se[ek out Šamaš(?);] I will appe[al] to your god;
79	I will pr[ay to Anu,] father of the gods, [in your presence(?);]
80	[may] Enlil the great counsellor [hear my] pray[ers] in your presence;
81	I will make suppli[cation ... ;]
82	I will fashion your statue in [gol]d without measure!

[Co]me, Šamhat — I will fix your destiny,
103	a [des]tiny that will not end for ever and ever.
104	[I will] curse you with the great curse;
105	quick, at once, may my curses close on you!
106	[May you never] make a household of your delight;
107	[may you never(?)] love [ ... ] of your favors(?);
108	[may you never s]it in the young women's parlor;
109	may the ground foul yo[ur] fin[e clothing;]
110	[may the drunkard] smear [your festival gown] in the [dus]t;
111	[may you never own a house ... ] and fair things,
112	[nor ... ] of the potter;
113	[ ... ] may you own nothing;
114	[bed, cha]ir, table — the pride of a household — may none be laid in your house;
115	[may the couch of your de]light be a doorway-bench;
116	[may the crossroa]ds of the highway be your dwelling-places,
117	[the waste plots be] your [sl]eeping-places, the shadow of the rampart your standing-place;
118	[may thornbush and br]iar flay your feet;
119	[may drunkard and s]ober alike slap your cheek;
120	[may ... ] the mistress-of-lawsuits(?) shout against you;
121	may no builder plaster [the roof of your house;]
122	[in your bedroom(?) may] the owl [r]oost;
123	[at your table may] no banquet [ever be l]aid;
127	[ ... pu]rple [fine]ry [ ... ]
128	[ ... ] the lap [ ... ]
129	whose lap [ ... ] —
130	because [you diminished] me, [the clean one,]
131	yes me, the clean one — [you diminish]ed me, out on my steppe!

Why, Enkidu, do you curse the harl[o]t Šamhat —
135	she who fed you bread fit for a god,
136	poured you ale fit for a king,
137	dressed you in a great garment,
138	and got you for companion the fine Gilgameš — you?
139	And [n]ow Gilgameš, the friend, your own brother,
140	[will l]ay you out on a great bed;
141	[o]n a bed of honor he will lay you out;
142	[he will s]eat you in the seat of peace, the seat at the left hand;
143	[the pri]nces of the earth will kiss your feet;
144	[he will make] the people of Uruk [we]ep for you, mourn for you;
145	[the pleasure-girls,] the people, he will fill with grieving for your sake;
146	[and h]e, after you, will carry a body matted with filth;
147	[he will pu]t on the skin of a lion and rove the op[en land.]

C[ome,] Š[amhat — I will fix your destiny:]
152	may [my] m[outh that cur]sed you turn and bl[ess you.]
153	May [govern]ors and princes fall in love with y[ou;]
154	may [the man one league off] be slapping his thigh,
155	the m[an two leagues off] shaking out his hair;
156	[may] no soldier [hold back fr]om you — may he undo his belt for you;
157	may he g[ive you] obsidian, lapis lazuli, and gold;
158	ear[rin]gs of filigree be his gift to you;
159	(may you enter the house of) a ma[n whose stores are secure,] whose heaps of grain are piled;
160	may I[štar, the ablest] of gods, bring you in, you yourself;
161	[for your sake may] the mother of seven, the first wife, be aband[oned!]
